WHY BUILD/TEST ARE RED - PRE-EXISTING, OUT-OF-SCOPE CROSS-WIDGET TYPE COLLISIONS
  The integrated solution build fails with 50x CS0101/CS0111 duplicate-definition
  errors that have NOTHING to do with this surface. Two previously-shipped widget
  PAIRS each declare the SAME public types in the SAME namespace
  (TeslaSync.App.DashboardWidgets), which is illegal in a single assembly:
    (a) EnergyFlowAnimatedWidget (0045)  vs  EnergyFlowWidget (0046)
        9 colliding types: EnergyFlowState, EnergyFlowSnapshot, EnergyFlowSize,
        EnergyFlowNode, EnergyFlowArrow, EnergyFlowDisplay, EnergyFlowProjection,
        EnergyFlowGeometry, EnergyFlowResultMapper.
    (b) FleetStatsBarWidget (0050)       vs  FleetStatsWidget (0051)
        1 colliding type: FleetStatsResultMapper.
  Only EnergyFlowWidget.Model.cs and FleetStatsWidget.Model.cs are flagged in the
  build log above; MediaNowPlayingWidget appears in ZERO build/test/format errors.
  These four widgets are OUT OF SCOPE ("Out of Scope: Other surfaces ... Delegation:
  FORBIDDEN"; Allowed files = MediaNowPlayingWidget.* + the log). Each pair member was
  green in its OWN isolated worktree (sibling logs 0046/0051 record BUILD_EXIT=0); the
  collision only surfaces in an integrated worktree containing both members of a pair
  (this one). De-duplicating them is the integration layer's responsibility, not this
  surface's. The XAML "WMC9999 Object reference" internal error in the raw build output
  is a CASCADE of the same C# duplicate-definition failure (proven below: once the
  duplicates are excluded the XAML markup compiler succeeds and the app builds clean).

